{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the complete DESIGN/documentation publication at 726474e3a0f6a6280731e0c0f669e049a9ecc52f, tree b553606d965e70deace48a117d72d6a136f3f750, primary 2ebe49bc0bce08790c404b6360067069bf67221b.\n\nAll six requested areas are present. The selected one-handle/callback API, snapshots plus invalidations, common admission boundary, separate recorded request custody, transport-independent durability, visible live reset and owned implementation sequence are coherent. Essential chosen contracts still need these corrections before the note can serve as the complete builder handoff.\n\nP2 — Define watch establishment, identity and snapshot correlation (note lines 526–568). A watch request supplies a request ID and target/selection. Its named result/View has no returned subscription ID, while notices and close require a watch ID and snapshot parts carry it only optionally. No rule assigns that ID, equates it to the initiating ID, or orders acknowledgment before parts/notices. Choose one explicit assignment and closed watch-open result with full binding, selection and initial position. State acknowledgment/part/notice ordering and bounded assembly identity/count/bytes/digest/position validation. The serialized snapshot/register-at-N rule is useful but does not supply the missing wire linkage. Preserve concurrent watch cleanup and incomplete/gap honesty.\n\nP2 — Close the selected common operation and authentication schemas (481–495, 538, 555–567, 646–671). ApplicationPort still lacks actual method signatures; OperationResult claims an operation-specific union without concrete watch-open/wait/closed/auth acknowledgment/refusal schemas, and the fixed authentication frame is literally auth:{...}. Later LiveChallenge, SignedLiveSessionRequest and LiveIntent make substantial choices; this is not absence of session design. Finish the exact read-session versus live-credential auth variants, refresh request/result, resolved MAC claim and bounded public hibernation-attachment shapes, plus operation-keyed success/refusal/timeout/reset/close results. Define permitted position disclosure and cancellation meanings. Keep private credentials, native signatures and existing authority owners separate. These essential public semantics belong in this design; builder should choose internal layout rather than infer another protocol architecture.\n\nP2 — Make live value and query result types agree with the selected language (62, 93, 149–174, 652–655). Native FieldValue/FieldRecord exclude null recursively, yet Selection.arguments and LiveIntent.fields use it while promising explicit nullable live fields without changing native types. The shared presence language permits clearing status/note with explicit null, distinct from omission. Select a separate concrete recursive live-value/schema mapping and carry it through signed fields, query arguments and live ActInput, preserving canonical null/omission/empty-list meaning and the old native envelope. Also, read(selection?:Selection) always promises StateOf\u003cD\u003e (native Summary or LiveViewValue), while named queries return arbitrary declared projections. Add the corresponding summary/query result overload or declared-query mapping within the same small interface; do not type a projected query as Summary. The watch overload already recognizes that distinction.\n\nP2 — Preserve the exact native pin in recorded positions (55–58 versus 517–520). The common port accepts recorded TargetBinding.definition as Digest|PlatformDefinition, matching actual native Summary, but Position.definition permits only Digest. Reads and waits on a platform target have no specified honest position representation. Carry the actual native pin union through recorded positions and their validators; do not silently hash a platform name into a different definition identity or remove the requested native scope path. This is a design interoperability contradiction, not a reproduced deployed failure.\n\nP3 — Correct the existing recorded settlement route at 508 to /settle. Current HTTP client and Worker have no /settlements alias. After closing the remaining schemas, update the early “remains to be completed” sentence to match the final selected contract.\n\nRetain the strong recovery choices: exact signed envelope/grants/side bytes plus binding retained and read back before first send; no send when custody cannot be retained; first directly correlated trusted refusal distinct from recovering a lost refusal; prior unknown not erased by a later refusal; native settle/receipt verification independent of watched heads; no transport failover, fresh signing or speech replay after uncertainty. Preserve captured expected revisions and context, complete/partial/stale versus communication and invocation outcomes, permission checks before private parts, native token windows versus proposed live sessions, and deliberate rejoin after G loss.\n\nBounded anonymous official excerpts support the socket/memory distinction in the note ([Cloudflare WebSockets](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/)) and the dated optional notification mechanisms ([MCP 2026 resources](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/resources), [MCP 2025 resources](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/resources)). They establish no actual negotiated host, Artroom reset, native acceptance or agent scheduling. Parked MCP remains inactive and the first explicit read/wait path must remain useful without notifications.\n\nRoot and two independent readers each freshly FULL read the whole 809-line BODY and complete native U3 through EOF: 2 units / 15 bounded displays / 98857 bytes / 1624 LF per reader. Whole three-reader native audit PASS exit 0: bb2fe8ecf1bcc26b188de29f8cc486f279fbb1b018adab706b3194fa99111935. Root FULL read the entire original six-section request, all five final seals/supplements and nine fresh native PARTIAL spans, 15621 bytes. Final ownership/seal/EOF/native-correspondence audit PASS exit 0 before verdict: 34a978a18cbeb3a01f18069e53c5953431e8958b7699cbee3f80647eee3ba3fe. Oversized/combined metadata or redisplay deviations and the overlapping auxiliary span receive no primary credit; fresh bounded reads and audited coverage stand.\n\nOnly this authored note differs from published 25467; packages, scripts and package/config bytes were verified unchanged. No project execution, typecheck, tests, gate, source edits, generated scenarios or private/provider/browser/process probes were performed by checker. This is the normal Git-bound document review under original 785a544715e7bc45e4e5e35edc7bbe78e9f289dc / producer 0b4676c6d0b15512f497fe3590246545545daf7d; evidence_only is omitted. It does not adopt the proposed API/wire/grammar or commission runtime implementation. Full scope, existing 4073/578/F1/W1/U1 ownership, source/gate/hosting/device/R4 acceptance and implementation duties remain open.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2ebe49bc0bce08790c404b6360067069bf67221b","binding":"assigned","head":"726474e3a0f6a6280731e0c0f669e049a9ecc52f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1abcac6b194db1aa0d9d99c794c420fb38410fd3\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0b4676c6d0b15512f497fe3590246545545daf7d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:28be663967e0739396181fbcc3cc9e1408fdc66b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}